      "updated": 1785917219155,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n1. Whenever an instance (non-static) method is called, the\nJVM implicitly passes the reference (address) of the calling\nobject to that method.\n\n2. The instance method receives this reference in a special\nreference variable called `this`.\n\n3. The `this` reference points to the current (calling)\nobject and remains available throughout the execution of\nthe method.\n\n4. Using `this`, an instance method can access the\ninstance variables and instance methods of the current\nobject.\n\n5. Every object has its own `this` reference. Therefore,\nwhen different objects call the same method, `this` refers\nto the respective calling object.\n\n\nTrue/False\n\n1. Does all methods have this keyword\n--> false, static methods do  not have this keyword as\nthey are called by class name not object ref , as we know\n \" this \" keeps the address of calling object .\n\n\n\nBefore JDK 25 :\n===============\n\npublic static void main ( String [] args ) \n{\n     SOP(this); // \n}\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nFROM JDK 25\n=============\nclass{\n void main () ---> known as instance main method\n{\n     SOP(this); //\n}\n}    \n\njvm will internally create Test class object .\n\n\nBenefits of this keyword\n=======================\n\n1. It resolve the overlapping of instance variable done by\nlocal variable within a method by same name .\n\n2. We can perform construction chaining .\n\n\n\nConstruction chaining\n===================\n\n1. Constructor chaining is the process of calling one\nconstructor from another constructor of the same class.\n\n2. Constructor chaining is done using `this()`.\n\n3. `this()` calls another constructor of the same class.\n\n4. `this()` must always be the first statement inside a\nconstructor.\n\n5. Only one constructor call (`this()` or `super()`) is\nallowed as the first statement in a constructor.\n\n6. Constructor chaining helps to avoid code duplication and\nimproves code reusability.\n\n7. Constructor chaining works only inside constructors.\n\n** this() is not a method. It is a special constructor\ninvocation used to call another constructor of the same\nclass.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n1. Whenever an instance (non-static) method is called, the JVM implicitly passes the reference (address) of the calling object to that method.\n\n2. The instance method receives this reference in a special reference variable called `this`.\n\n3. The `this` reference points to the current (calling) object and remains available throughout the execution of the method.\n\n4. Using `this`, an instance method can access the instance variables and instance methods of the current object.\n\n5. Every object has its own `this` reference. Therefore, when different objects call the same method, `this` refers to the respective calling object.\n\n\nTrue/False\n\n1. Does all methods have this keyword\n--> false, static methods do  not have this keyword as they are called by class name not object ref , as we know\n \" this \" keeps the address of calling object .\n\n\n\nBefore JDK 25 :\n===============\n\npublic static void main ( String [] args ) \n{\n     SOP(this); // \n}\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nFROM JDK 25\n=============\nclass{\n void main () ---> known as instance main method\n{\n     SOP(this); //\n}\n}    \n\njvm will internally create Test class object .\n\n\nBenefits of this keyword\n=======================\n\n1. It resolve the overlapping of instance variable done by local variable within a method by same name .\n\n2. We can perform construction chaining .\n\n\n\nConstruction chaining\n===================\n\n1. Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor of the same class.\n\n2. Constructor chaining is done using `this()`.\n\n3. `this()` calls another constructor of the same class.\n\n4. `this()` must always be the first statement inside a constructor.\n\n5. Only one constructor call (`this()` or `super()`) is allowed as the first statement in a constructor.\n\n6. Constructor chaining helps to avoid code duplication and improves code reusability.\n\n7. Constructor chaining works only inside constructors.\n\n** this() is not a method. It is a special constructor invocation used to call another constructor of the same class.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
